2026-06-30 20:12:28,128 [INFO] ("Polity population measures the estimated number of people residing within the territory of a polity, often reconstructed through archaeological proxies—such as urban density, trade networks, and agricultural irrigation systems—as well as historical census data or chroniclers' estimates. \n\nFor the polity <polity> during the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, determine the estimated population range. Your response must be formatted strictly as a JSON array containing only the numeric values representing the start and end of the range, without the unit 'people': [<start_of_range>, <end_of_range>].", None, [{'role': 'system', 'content': "You are are history professor writing challenging questions for PhD students.\n\n    Your task is to create a challenging yet well-defined historical exam question based on a variable definition from a historical dataset.\n    Follow the rules below, and return ONLY the final question template, based on the examples provided. \n\n    1. Begin the question with a concise explanation of the variable's definition, strictly adhering to the provided description, if no description is provided, use the variable name as the basis for the question.\n    2. Ensure all of the following template markers are used:\n        - '<polity>' for polity names (e.g. 'The Papal States')\n        - '<time-start>' and '<time-end>' for temporal scope\n    3. The question should make abundantly clear what the format of the desired response is and that only that response should be given.\n    4. Specify the answer unit if applicable, but require that the final answer does not contain it.\n    5. The answer format should be requested as: [<start_of_range>, <end_of_range>], just like a json array\n\n    Provided variables, and description will always relate to a numeric value, so ensure that questions refer to identifying a range.\n    Structure your response with a clear question template, and template markers.\n    "}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "\n                        Variable name: administrative level\n                        Variable unit: not applicable\n                        Variable description: Talking about Hierarchical Complexity, Administrative levels records the administrative levels of a polity. An example of hierarchy for a state society could be (1) the overall ruler, (2) provincial/regional governors, (3) district heads, (4) town mayors, (5) village heads. Note that unlike in settlement hierarchy, here you code people hierarchy. Do not simply copy settlement hierarchy data here. For archaeological polities, you will usually code as 'unknown', unless experts identified ranks of chiefs or officials independently of the settlement hierarchy. Note: Often there are more than one concurrent administrative hierarchy. In the example above the hierarchy refers to the territorial government. In addition, the ruler may have a hierarchically organized central bureaucracy located in the capital. For example, (4)the overall ruler, (3) chiefs of various ministries, (2) midlevel bureaucrats, (1) scribes and clerks. In the narrative paragraph detail what is known about both hierarchies. The machine-readable code should reflect the largest number (the longer chain of command).\n                    "}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Given the concept of Hierarchical Complexity in terms of Administrative Levels, identify the maximum number of administrative levels for the polity <polity> during the period from <time-start> to <time-end>. An example of hierarchy for a state society could be (1) the overall ruler, (2) provincial/regional governors, (3) district heads, (4) town mayors, (5) village heads. Note that unlike in settlement hierarchy, here you code people hierarchy. Provide your answer in the format [<start_of_range>, <end_of_range>].'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '\n                        Variable name: military level\n                        Variable unit: not applicable\n                        Variable description: Talking about Hierarchical Complexity, Military levels records the Military levels of a polity. Same principle as with Administrative levels. Start with the commander-in-chief coded as: level 1, and work down to the private. Even in primitive societies such as simple chiefdoms it is often possible to distinguish at least two levels – a commander and soldiers. A complex chiefdom would be coded three levels. The presence of warrior burials might be the basis for inferring the existence of a military organization. (The lowest military level is always the individual soldier).\n                    '}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "Using the definition of 'Military Level' as a measure of hierarchical complexity within a military structure, identify the range of military levels present in the polity <polity> between <time-start> and <time-end>. Your response should reflect the levels of hierarchy from the commander-in-chief to the individual soldier and should be expressed as a JSON array in the format: [<start_of_range>, <end_of_range>]."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '\n                        Variable name: polity territory\n                        Variable unit: square kilometres\n                        Variable description: Talking about Social Scale, Polity territory is coded in squared kilometers.\n                    '}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "Based on the concept of 'Polity Territory' as defined in terms of the area a polity controlled in squared kilometers, provide the range of the area <polity> governed from <time-start> to <time-end>. Please provide your answer in the format: [<start_of_range>, <end_of_range>], without the unit."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '\n                        Variable name: polity population\n                        Variable unit: people\n                        Variable description: Evidence of irrigation and the flourishing trade network seems to indicate a growth of population in the region controlled by the Indo-Greek Kingdom. However, this is largely speculative based on the current archaeological record. The description of Bactria as \'the land of a thousand cities\' does seem to imply a relatively dense population. So far, only two of these have seen extensive excavation. There is also evidence that Bactria was fertile and extensively irrigated. <br>McEvedy and Jones <br>200 BCE: Russian Turkestan: 1,000,000; Afghanistan 1,750,000. Highest density of population likely to be in the Greco-Bactrian region which contained cities.<br>126 BCE the Chinese chronicler Zhang Qian estimated "some 1,000,000 or more persons."\n                    '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 2195, 'temperature': 0.2})
2026-06-30 20:12:33,307 [INFO] ("Based on the concept of 'Polity Population'—defined as the total size of a polity's population, which is estimated using historical demographic data, warrior counts (extrapolating total population from military strength and extended family groups), and regional population estimates while accounting for fluctuations due to tribal affiliations and political fortunes—identify the population range for the polity <polity> during the period from <time-start> to <time-end>. Your response must be specified in terms of the number of people, but the final answer must not contain the unit. Provide your answer strictly in the format: [<start_of_range>, <end_of_range>].", None, [{'role': 'system', 'content': "You are are history professor writing challenging questions for PhD students.\n\n    Your task is to create a challenging yet well-defined historical exam question based on a variable definition from a historical dataset.\n    Follow the rules below, and return ONLY the final question template, based on the examples provided. \n\n    1. Begin the question with a concise explanation of the variable's definition, strictly adhering to the provided description, if no description is provided, use the variable name as the basis for the question.\n    2. Ensure all of the following template markers are used:\n        - '<polity>' for polity names (e.g. 'The Papal States')\n        - '<time-start>' and '<time-end>' for temporal scope\n    3. The question should make abundantly clear what the format of the desired response is and that only that response should be given.\n    4. Specify the answer unit if applicable, but require that the final answer does not contain it.\n    5. The answer format should be requested as: [<start_of_range>, <end_of_range>], just like a json array\n\n    Provided variables, and description will always relate to a numeric value, so ensure that questions refer to identifying a range.\n    Structure your response with a clear question template, and template markers.\n    "}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "\n                        Variable name: administrative level\n                        Variable unit: not applicable\n                        Variable description: Talking about Hierarchical Complexity, Administrative levels records the administrative levels of a polity. An example of hierarchy for a state society could be (1) the overall ruler, (2) provincial/regional governors, (3) district heads, (4) town mayors, (5) village heads. Note that unlike in settlement hierarchy, here you code people hierarchy. Do not simply copy settlement hierarchy data here. For archaeological polities, you will usually code as 'unknown', unless experts identified ranks of chiefs or officials independently of the settlement hierarchy. Note: Often there are more than one concurrent administrative hierarchy. In the example above the hierarchy refers to the territorial government. In addition, the ruler may have a hierarchically organized central bureaucracy located in the capital. For example, (4)the overall ruler, (3) chiefs of various ministries, (2) midlevel bureaucrats, (1) scribes and clerks. In the narrative paragraph detail what is known about both hierarchies. The machine-readable code should reflect the largest number (the longer chain of command).\n                    "}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Given the concept of Hierarchical Complexity in terms of Administrative Levels, identify the maximum number of administrative levels for the polity <polity> during the period from <time-start> to <time-end>. An example of hierarchy for a state society could be (1) the overall ruler, (2) provincial/regional governors, (3) district heads, (4) town mayors, (5) village heads. Note that unlike in settlement hierarchy, here you code people hierarchy. Provide your answer in the format [<start_of_range>, <end_of_range>].'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '\n                        Variable name: military level\n                        Variable unit: not applicable\n                        Variable description: Talking about Hierarchical Complexity, Military levels records the Military levels of a polity. Same principle as with Administrative levels. Start with the commander-in-chief coded as: level 1, and work down to the private. Even in primitive societies such as simple chiefdoms it is often possible to distinguish at least two levels – a commander and soldiers. A complex chiefdom would be coded three levels. The presence of warrior burials might be the basis for inferring the existence of a military organization. (The lowest military level is always the individual soldier).\n                    '}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "Using the definition of 'Military Level' as a measure of hierarchical complexity within a military structure, identify the range of military levels present in the polity <polity> between <time-start> and <time-end>. Your response should reflect the levels of hierarchy from the commander-in-chief to the individual soldier and should be expressed as a JSON array in the format: [<start_of_range>, <end_of_range>]."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '\n                        Variable name: polity territory\n                        Variable unit: square kilometres\n                        Variable description: Talking about Social Scale, Polity territory is coded in squared kilometers.\n                    '}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "Based on the concept of 'Polity Territory' as defined in terms of the area a polity controlled in squared kilometers, provide the range of the area <polity> governed from <time-start> to <time-end>. Please provide your answer in the format: [<start_of_range>, <end_of_range>], without the unit."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '\n                        Variable name: polity population\n                        Variable unit: people\n                        Variable description: The total size of the Hephthalite population is unknown. In the territory of Tokharistan there were reportedly 5,000-6,000 Hephthalite warriors. This could suggest a population of 50,000 individuals if the extended family groups were included, although this would have been experienced considerable ﬂuctuations with the increase and decrease of the number of affiliated tribes and fortune of the Hepthalite. <br>McEvedy and Jones (1978) 26,500,000: 500 CE <br>Russian Tukestan 1,000,000: 400-600 CE<br>Pakistan, India and Bangladesh: 45,000,000: 400 CE; 50,000,000: 500 CE. In 200 BC approximately 40% in the Ganges Basin which would translate to 20m in 500 CE. Hephthalites held Upper and Middle Gangers Basin so perhaps two-thirds (13m) under Hephthalite control. Indus Basin likely to have been next most populous part of Indian sub-continent at this time. If 25% of the total: 12.5m.\n                    '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 2263, 'temperature': 0.2})
